### 1. Scope, definitions and interpretation

**1.1 Licensed Material.** "Software" means the copyrightable source code,
documentation, modifications and other material expressly made available under
this License, whether in source, object, WebAssembly or another form. This
License applies only to rights the relevant copyright holder is legally
entitled to grant.

**1.2 Parties.** "You" means any person or legal entity exercising rights
under this License. "Copyright Holder" means a person or entity controlling
relevant copyright rights. "Organization" means Toko Studios Ltd, the
specifically named legal entity, and does not include any other person or
entity merely because of a relationship with the Organization or a similar
name.

**1.3 Live Deployment.** A "Live Deployment" is the use of Software to support
operational workloads on the Internet Computer Protocol or a functional fork
or adaptation of that protocol. It includes public, private, enterprise,
sovereign, permissioned and self-hosted environments, whether operated
commercially or internally.

Isolated development replicas, simulators and test environments used
exclusively for non-operational development, validation or testing are
excluded. An environment serving genuine operational workloads is not excluded
merely because it is described as experimental, internal or a test.

**1.4 Cloud Engine.** A "Cloud Engine" is a Live Deployment using dedicated or
reserved execution capacity whose node selection, capacity allocation or
operational control is determined for the benefit of a particular customer,
enterprise or affiliated group.

This includes environments described as Cloud Engines, sovereign subnets,
private subnets, dedicated execution environments, enterprise clouds and
equivalent deployments using the Internet Computer Protocol or a functional
fork or adaptation.

Classification depends on the actual technical and commercial arrangement, not
branding, ownership, payment currency, network connectivity or provider
identity.

An ordinary shared public Internet Computer subnet does not become a Cloud
Engine merely because its canisters contain private data.

**1.5 Covered Capacity.** "Covered Capacity" means the smallest objectively
identifiable, separately metered and commercially priced infrastructure
capacity unit used to host or execute Software in a Cloud Engine.

If an engine is supplied and priced only as an indivisible reservation, the
entire reservation is Covered Capacity. A smaller allocation may be used only
where independent metering and genuine resource separation support it.

Artificial partitioning, nominal resource limits, related-party arrangements
or accounting allocations that do not reflect actual infrastructure use must
not reduce Covered Capacity.

**1.6 Infrastructure Base.** "Infrastructure Base" means the gross
consideration payable, or independently determined market value where
applicable, for Covered Capacity during the relevant accounting period.

It includes reserved nodes, compute, storage, network capacity, bandwidth,
infrastructure availability commitments and infrastructure provided in kind.
It includes amounts retained by providers, intermediaries or affiliates,
without deductions for their operating costs, margins, commissions or profit.

Separately itemized statutory taxes and genuinely separate non-infrastructure
services are excluded.

Infrastructure Base does not mean application revenue, customer deposits, user
assets, token supply, enterprise turnover or unrelated infrastructure.

**1.7 Qualifying ICP Burn.** A "Qualifying ICP Burn" is the actual,
irreversible destruction of native ICP on the canonical Internet Computer
mainnet through its recognized ICP-to-cycles conversion mechanism.

It must be independently verifiable through the canonical ICP ledger and
relevant Cycles Minting Canister records, or their official protocol
successors.

A mere transfer, token lock, staking transaction, wrapped-token destruction,
simulated burn, locally created resource credit or destruction of cycles
without verifiable originating ICP destruction is not independently a
Qualifying ICP Burn.

**1.8 Accounting Period.** An "Accounting Period" is one UTC calendar month,
including a partial month when deployment begins or ends.

