### 3. Shared-network deployment requirement

For any Live Deployment that is not a Cloud Engine, permission to operate the
Software is conditional on all protocol resource credits used to pay the
deployment's resource charges being funded by Qualifying ICP Burns.

Where resources are charged in cycles, all cycles applied to those charges
must originate from qualifying mainnet ICP-to-cycles conversion, directly or
through verifiably funded transfers or pools.

The requirement applies to 100% of resource funding, not to application
revenue, assets held for users or tokens unrelated to infrastructure
consumption.

Previously completed Qualifying ICP Burns may satisfy this condition. The
License does not require a second burn where resource credits have already
been funded through qualifying conversion.

Credits created exclusively through local simulation, unauthorized local
issuance or an alternative process without qualifying mainnet ICP backing do
not satisfy this condition for operational use.

Provider-supplied and pooled cycles are permitted subject to the allocation
and evidence requirements in Section 5.

### 4. Cloud Engine deployment requirement

**4.1 Minimum burn.** Permission to operate the Software on a Cloud Engine is
expressly conditional on Qualifying ICP Burns having an independently
verifiable value of at least twenty percent (20%) of the Infrastructure Base
attributable to its Covered Capacity.

The threshold applies to gross infrastructure consideration or value, not
merely the operator's accounting profit or the portion retained as a protocol
fee.

**4.2 Existing burns.** Qualifying ICP Burns already performed to fund that
Cloud Engine may count toward this threshold, including qualifying conversions
performed by an infrastructure provider on the operator's behalf.

No additional burn is required to the extent that eligible, unallocated
Qualifying ICP Burns already meet the threshold.

Destruction or consumption of cycles alone is not evidence that the
corresponding ICP burn occurred.

**4.3 Free, discounted and affiliated capacity.** Free capacity, introductory
discounts, subsidies, internal infrastructure, affiliated-provider
arrangements and in-kind consideration do not reduce the Infrastructure Base
below an independently supportable arm's-length value for comparable capacity.

Where comparable capacity is offered at an independently published price, that
price establishes a minimum reference valuation after reasonable, documented
adjustments for capacity, geography, availability and service level.

If no reasonably comparable public tariff exists, the operator must obtain a
written valuation from an independent, appropriately qualified infrastructure
valuation professional before operational use. The valuation must state its
assumptions, capacity specification, comparison methodology and applicable
period.

No discretionary approval from Toko Studios Ltd is required solely to
establish an independently supported valuation under this clause.

**4.4 Currency conversion.** Infrastructure Base and qualifying burn values
must be reconciled in Special Drawing Rights (XDR).

Infrastructure amounts denominated in other currencies must be converted using
independently published rates appropriate to the relevant settlement date.

The value of ICP destroyed through qualifying conversion must be calculated
using the actual applicable mainnet ICP/XDR conversion rate for that
conversion.

The operator must disclose the rate, timestamp, source, calculation and
rounding method used. Private exchange-rate arrangements cannot substitute for
independently verifiable conversion evidence.

**4.5 Advance coverage.** Before commencing Cloud Engine operational use, the
operator must establish qualifying burn coverage of at least 20% of the
Infrastructure Base for the initial prepaid or reserved Covered Capacity.

Additional reserved capacity must be covered before it becomes operational.

Variable or metered infrastructure consumption must remain within a
documented, prepaid qualifying-burn coverage limit. The operator must obtain
additional burn coverage before consumption exceeds that limit.

A later reconciliation does not retrospectively authorize operation that
lacked the required coverage at the time.

**4.6 Monthly reconciliation.** Within thirty (30) days after each Accounting
Period, the operator must reconcile actual Infrastructure Base, advance burn
coverage, additional qualifying burns and remaining allocated coverage.

The reconciliation must identify and correct overallocations, valuation
differences and changes in actual capacity. Surplus qualifying coverage may be
carried forward only where it is demonstrably unused, specifically allocated
and not counted elsewhere.

Deployment closure, provider substitution or transfer does not extinguish
responsibility for earlier unauthorized use or other accrued liabilities.

### 5. Verification, allocation and evidence

**5.1 Public audit trail.** Every Live Deployment must have a publicly
accessible, independently verifiable evidence record sufficient to establish
the qualifying resource funding or burn coverage required by this License.

For shared-network deployments, the record may reference verifiable protocol
funding records and properly reconciled provider pools rather than repeating
upstream burn transactions.

For Cloud Engines, the record must include:

a. A stable deployment identifier and identification of the applicable Covered
Capacity.

b. The Accounting Period and applicable capacity specification.

c. The Infrastructure Base, valuation source and calculation methodology.

d. Any relevant currency conversions and rates.

e. Canonical mainnet ICP ledger transaction or block references and evidence
connecting those transactions to qualifying ICP destruction.

f. The amount and XDR value of each allocated Qualifying ICP Burn.

g. The allocation of burns to the relevant deployment, capacity and Accounting
Period.

h. An accounting of advance coverage, actual consumption, reconciliation
adjustments and remaining unused coverage.

**5.2 Publication timing.** Cloud Engine identity, initial valuation, funding
methodology and initial qualifying burn evidence must be publicly available
before operational use begins.

Subsequent accounting records must be published within the reconciliation
deadline established in Section 4.6.

**5.3 Pooled funding.** A provider may fund multiple deployments using pooled
Qualifying ICP Burns, provided each allocation is independently traceable and
reconcilable.

The total value allocated from any burn must not exceed its independently
verified qualifying value. The same value may not be counted more than once.

Provider-generated statements, invoices, screenshots, dashboards and local
cycle-consumption counters do not, without supporting mainnet evidence,
establish a Qualifying ICP Burn.

**5.4 Confidentiality and privacy.** Evidence may use stable pseudonymous
deployment identifiers and may exclude personal information, application
contents, credentials, trade secrets and other information unnecessary to
demonstrate compliance.

Redaction, aggregation or pseudonymization must not prevent independent
verification of qualifying burn totals, relevant capacity valuation,
allocations or accounting completeness.

**5.5 Record preservation.** Required records must remain accessible for at
least three (3) years after the Accounting Period to which they relate.
Corrections must preserve an auditable history of material changes.

